1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,950
[ MUSIC ]

2
00:00:03,970 --> 00:00:13,820
The Power of Studying Combustion on the ISS - presented by Science@NASA 

3
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:21,660
If you wanted to reduce the amount of pollution humans produce, where might you look? 

4
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:24,440
Here? Yep.

5
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:25,950
Here? Sure.

6
00:00:25,970 --> 00:00:30,210
Here? Wait, what? 

7
00:00:30,230 --> 00:00:32,830
That's exactly where scientists and researchers are looking: 

8
00:00:32,850 --> 00:00:37,090
about 250 miles above Earth, on the International Space Station (ISS).

9
00:00:37,110 --> 00:00:42,840
Here, astronauts are working on a series of experiments to learn more about combustion.

10
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:50,690
Why combustion? Dennis Stocker, project scientist for the Advanced Combustion via Microgravity Experiments, or ACME, says

11
00:00:50,710 --> 00:00:57,750
"Consider this. About 85 percent of the energy we use comes from combustion - the burning of some sort of fuel."

12
00:00:57,770 --> 00:01:05,250
About 70% of the electricity we use at home and work comes from power plants that use combustion.

13
00:01:05,270 --> 00:01:10,430
The combustion of fuels powers the engines in many modes of transportation.

14
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:15,580
Stocker continues: "If we can learn more about the process, we might be able to find

15
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:19,320
newer and less polluting ways to burn fuel."

16
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:23,700
Three series of combustion experiments taking place on the International Space Station's

17
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:29,160
Combustion Integration Rack (CIR) are looking at combustion in very different ways.

18
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:34,400
The Flame Extinguishment Experiment, or FLEX, examined the burning of a single droplet of fuel

19
00:01:34,420 --> 00:01:38,850
in the microgravity confines of the ISS.

20
00:01:38,870 --> 00:01:46,350
The last of the droplet combustion experiments were conducted in September 2017. ACME began in November 2017.

21
00:01:46,370 --> 00:01:53,130
ACME is composed of six major experiments, with each taking about 5 months to complete.

22
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:58,620
These experiments will examine how various gasses burn without gravity affecting the flames.

23
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:06,940
The Solid Fuel Ignition and Extinction or SoFIE experiment, will explore the properties of burning solid matter.  

24
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:09,990
So why study combustion in micro gravity?

25
00:02:10,010 --> 00:02:15,760
As Stocker explains: "On the ISS, you have conditions that you can't replicate on Earth.

26
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:20,720
For example, consider a candle when it's burning. The flame flickers.

27
00:02:20,740 --> 00:02:27,230
Why? Hot gasses from the flame rise while cooler, denser air is pulled to the bottom of the flame.

28
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:32,580
This flow occurs at high speeds, resulting in turbulence and instability.

29
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:36,170
That instability makes it hard to study the properties of the flame.

30
00:02:36,190 --> 00:02:43,390
But on the ISS, there's no flicker which allows us to make more precise and consistent measurements.

31
00:02:43,410 --> 00:02:47,750
If we have precise measurements we get closer to learning how to burn more efficiently."

32
00:02:47,770 --> 00:02:53,820
More efficiency could lead to real cost savings for producing power.

33
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:59,680
The research could also lead to reducing the production of soot and other pollutants.

34
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:04,700
Combustion experiments conducted in space have contributed to practical applications on Earth,

35
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:10,700
especially in the field of fire safety. A new type of fire extinguisher is just one example.

36
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:17,260
And, as Stocker notes: "I think we're in for a few more surprises as a result of these microgravity experiments."

37
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:21,800
That's because, not surprisingly, Stocker knew where to look for answers

38
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:26,310
to reducing the amount of global pollution: He looked up.

39
00:03:26,330 --> 00:03:31,610
For more from the International Space Station, go to www.nasa.gov/station.

40
00:03:31,630 --> 00:03:38,860
If you're burning to learn more about other hot science topics, visit science.nasa.gov.
